y average"], ["Hotel spelled another way", (~raw["hotel"].isin(HOTELS)).sum(), "A summary by hotel lists 7 hotels instead of 3"], ["Channel spelled another way", raw["channel"].isin(["OTA", "online travel agency", "Online Travel Agency"]).sum(), "OTA cancellations are split across 4 labels"], ["Rate of 0 USD", free.sum(), "A free room is not a price: it drags the average rate down"], ["Rate of 1,000 USD or more", huge.sum(), "One typo moves a hotel's average by several dollars"], ["Negative lead time", negative.sum(), "Booked after arrival: the dates were swapped"], ["Booking with 0 adults", no_adults.sum(), "A room needs a guest: the record is broken"], ["Missing children", raw["children"].isna().sum(), "Rows with gaps break some calculations and models"], ["Missing country", raw["country"].isna().sum(), "Market analyses by country would silently drop them"], ], columns=["problem", "rows", "why it matters for a decision"]) audit Out[10]: .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type { vertical-align: middle; } .dataframe tbody tr th { vertical-align: top; } .dataframe thead th { text-align: right; } problem rows why it matters for a decision 0 Duplicate rows 60 A booking counted twice weighs double in every average 1 Hotel spelled another way 256 A summary by hotel lists 7 hotels instead of 3 2 Channel spelled another way 134 OTA cancellations are split across 4 labels 3 Rate of 0 USD 12 A free room is not a price: it drags the average rate down 4 Rate of 1,000 USD or more 6 One typo moves a hotel's average by several dollars 5 Negative lead time 10 Booked after arrival: the dates were swapped 6 Booking with 0 adults 8 A room needs a guest: the record is broken 7 Missing children 61 Rows with gaps break some calculations and models 8 Missing country 184 Market analyses by country would silently drop them ☕ Break. In the second block you clean the file, check the result against Altamar's published clean file, and turn it into tables, charts and findings.

Clean data at last. Most management questions have the same shape: what is the average X for each Y? That is a groupby : split the bookings by Y, apply a calculation inside each group, combine the answers into one small table. ▶ Interactive simulator: open this session on the course website to use it. First the average nightly rate at each hotel, now with three hotels ( count says how many bookings stand behind each average). Then a pivot table , a groupby with two keys, one on the rows and one on the columns: average rate by hotel and room type. In [16]: Copied! rate_by_hotel = df . groupby ( "hotel" )[ "adr" ] . agg ([ "count" , "mean" , "median" ]) . round ( 1 ) print ( rate_by_hotel . loc [ HOTELS ], " 
