") cancel_by_channel = df.groupby("channel")["canceled"].mean().sort_values() for channel, rate in cancel_by_channel.items(): print(f"{channel: < 22} {rate:.1%}") Overall cancellation rate: 33.7% Corporate 15.6% Direct 29.4% Travel agent 35.5% Online travel agency 42.2% The same rate by channel and customer type, in percent. Empty cells ( NaN ) are combinations that don't exist: groups don't book through the corporate channel. In [18]: Copied! cancel_table = df . pivot_table ( index = "channel" , columns = "customer_type" , values = "canceled" , aggfunc = "mean" ) ( cancel_table * 100 ) . round ( 1 ) cancel_table = df.pivot_table(index="channel", columns="customer_type", values="canceled", aggfunc="mean") (cancel_table * 100).round(1) Out[18]: .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type { vertical-align: middle; } .dataframe tbody tr th { vertical-align: top; } .dataframe thead th { text-align: right; } customer_type Business Group Leisure channel Corporate 15.6 NaN NaN Direct 14.8 43.2 33.3 Online travel agency 17.4 62.8 44.6 Travel agent NaN 37.7 31.0 Last summary: when do guests arrive? We add the arrival month as a new column and count bookings per month and hotel with crosstab , a pivot table that counts. In [19]: Copied! df [ "arrival_month" ] = pd . to_datetime ( df [ "arrival_date" ]) . dt . month arrivals = pd . crosstab ( df [ "arrival_month" ], df [ "hotel" ])[ HOTELS ] arrivals . index = MONTHS arrivals . T df["arrival_month"] = pd.to_datetime(df["arrival_date"]).dt.month arrivals = pd.crosstab(df["arrival_month"], df["hotel"])[HOTELS] arrivals.index = MONTHS arrivals.T Out[19]: .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type { vertical-align: middle; } .dataframe tbody tr th { vertical-align: top; } .dataframe thead th { text-align: right; } Jan Feb Mar Apr May Jun Jul Aug Sep Oct Nov Dec hotel Santiago 141 91 211 202 228 219 187 216 233 267 271 183 Viña del Mar 459 369 180 92 88 83 84 82 106 123 143 213 Puerto Varas 249 230 116 76 60 66 200 88 72 84 107 145 Business takeaway: Viña del Mar receives more than five times as many arrivals in January (459) as in August (82); Santiago is busiest from September to November. Each hotel lives on its own calendar. {"q": "<code>df.groupby('channel')['canceled'].mean()</code> gives 0.422 for online travel agencies. What does 0.422 mean?", "options": ["0.422 bookings were cancelled", "42.2% of online travel agency bookings were cancelled", "Online travel agencies represent 42.2% of all cancellations", "The average booking was 42.2% cancelled"], "answer": 1, "why": "canceled is 1 or 0, so its mean inside a group is the share of 1s: the cancellation rate of that channel. The share of all cancellations that come from OTAs is a different number: it also depends on how many bookings each channel brings.", "code": false}
